   "dataCountsVerdict": "confirmed",
   "finding": "The ranking's ORDER held, but its MAGNITUDE did not. On 20 fresh endpoint pairs none of which they used, lazysp_alternate certified 14/20 and q2c_nomodel 14/20, against 13/20 for q2c, collective_shared and collective_isolated - the same ordering as their headline (lazysp = q2c_nomodel > q2c = both collectives), with zero false certificates for every policy. But LazySP's entire margin over q2c is ONE pair, VFVS->ILSG, and it is again a VFVS-start, top-variant-target pair: on the 14 pairs that do not start from VFVS, all five policies resolve exactly the same set, and no pair was resolved by LazySP alone. So the advantage is narrow and structurally concentrated, not broad, exactly as our recount of their own panel found (all 18 of their LazySP-only wins are VFVS-start: 17 VFVS->ALKG plus 1 VFVS->AIKG). The 18-point headline gap is largely a composition effect - 80 of their 100 episodes start from VFVS, versus 6 of my 20 - so on a panel that does not oversample that one family the gap shrinks to 5 points (1 pair). Two further points cut against the headline reading: q2c_nomodel ties LazySP at 14/20, so the GP surrogate is not what buys the win, and LazySP is strictly the least assay-efficient policy on this panel (mean 352.6 assays over its resolved episodes versus 149.2 for q2c), spending more assays than q2c on every single pair both resolved."
  },
